WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:04.550
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.550 --> 00:00:09.910
Approximately 5 months ago, we talked about
a technique called Neural Radiance Fields,

00:00:09.910 --> 00:00:16.080
or NERF in short, that worked on a 5D neural
radiance field representation.

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:18.310
So what does this mean exactly?

00:00:18.310 --> 00:00:23.699
What this means is that we have 3 dimensions
for location and two for view direction, or

00:00:23.699 --> 00:00:29.561
in short, the input is where we are in space
give it to a neural network to learn it, and

00:00:29.561 --> 00:00:35.381
synthesize new, previously unseen views of
not just the materials in the scene, but the

00:00:35.381 --> 00:00:37.440
entire scene itself.

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:43.010
In short, it can learn and reproduce entire
real-world scenes from only a few views by

00:00:43.010 --> 00:00:44.890
using neural networks.

00:00:44.890 --> 00:00:47.449
And the results were just out of this world.

00:00:47.449 --> 00:00:48.449
Look.

00:00:48.449 --> 00:00:53.460
It could deal with many kinds of matte and
glossy materials, and even refractions worked

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:54.850
quite well.

00:00:54.850 --> 00:01:00.809
It also understood depth so accurately that
we could use it for augmented reality applications

00:01:00.809 --> 00:01:05.489
where we put a new, virtual object in the
scene and it correctly determined whether

00:01:05.489 --> 00:01:10.270
it is in front of, or behind the real objects
in the scene.

00:01:10.270 --> 00:01:12.830
However, not everything was perfect.

00:01:12.830 --> 00:01:17.960
In many cases, it had trouble with scenes
with variable lighting conditions and lots

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:19.530
of occluders.

00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:22.390
You might ask, is that a problem?

00:01:22.390 --> 00:01:28.530
Well, imagine a use case of a tourist attraction
that a lot of people take photos of, and we

00:01:28.530 --> 00:01:34.299
then have a collection of photos taken during
a different time of the day, and of course,

00:01:34.299 --> 00:01:35.990
with a lot of people around.

00:01:35.990 --> 00:01:43.049
But, hey, remember that this means an application
where we have exactly these conditions: a

00:01:43.049 --> 00:01:47.200
wide variety of illumination changes and occluders.

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:50.110
This is exactly what NERF was not too good
at!

00:01:50.110 --> 00:01:53.409
Let’s see how it did on such a case.

00:01:53.409 --> 00:01:59.420
Yes, we see both abrupt changes in the illumination,
and the remnants of the folks occluding the

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:01.500
Brandenburg Gate as well.

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:05.740
And this is where this new technique from
scientists at Google Research by the name

00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:08.149
NERF-W shines.

00:02:08.149 --> 00:02:12.650
It takes such a photo collection, and tries
to reconstruct the whole scene from it, which

00:02:12.650 --> 00:02:16.190
we can, again, render from new viewpoints.

00:02:16.190 --> 00:02:20.260
So, how well did the new method do in this
case?

00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:22.379
Let’s see.

00:02:22.379 --> 00:02:23.450
Wow.

00:02:23.450 --> 00:02:26.480
Just look at how consistent those results
are.

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:29.930
So much improvement in just 6 months of time.

00:02:29.930 --> 00:02:32.230
This is unbelievable.

00:02:32.230 --> 00:02:35.760
This is how it did in a similar case with
the Trevi fountain.

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:37.760
Absolutely beautiful.

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:42.620
And what is even more beautiful is that since
it has variation in the viewpoint information,

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:47.560
we can change these viewpoints around as the
algorithm learned to reconstruct the scene

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:48.720
itself.

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:53.440
This is something that the original NERF technique
could also do, however, what it couldn’t

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:56.129
do, is the same, with illumination.

00:02:56.129 --> 00:03:01.190
Now, we can also change the lighting conditions
together with the viewpoint.

00:03:01.190 --> 00:03:06.590
This truly showcases a deep understanding
of illumination and geometry.

00:03:06.590 --> 00:03:09.049
That is not trivial at all!

00:03:09.049 --> 00:03:13.360
For instance, when loading this scene into
this neural re-rendering technique from last

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:18.959
year, it couldn’t tell whether we see just
color variations on the same geometry, or

00:03:18.959 --> 00:03:21.400
if the geometry itself is changing.

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:28.950
And, look, this new method does much better
on cases like this.

00:03:28.950 --> 00:03:29.950
So clean!

00:03:29.950 --> 00:03:35.160
Now that we have seen the images, let’s
see what the numbers say for these scenes.

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:39.611
The NRW is the neural re-rendering technique
we just saw, and the other one is the NERF

00:03:39.611 --> 00:03:40.611
paper from this year.

00:03:40.611 --> 00:03:46.459
The abbreviations show different ways of comparing
the output images, the up and down arrows

00:03:46.459 --> 00:03:50.260
show whether they are subject to maximization
or minimization.

00:03:50.260 --> 00:03:56.180
They are both relatively close, but, when
we look at the new method, we see one of the

00:03:56.180 --> 00:04:01.049
rare cases where it wins decisively regardless
of what we are measuring.

00:04:01.049 --> 00:04:02.049
Incredible.

00:04:02.049 --> 00:04:07.480
This paper truly is a great leap in just a
few months, but of course, not everything

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:08.480
is perfect here.

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:13.760
This technique may fail to reconstruct regions
that are only visible on just a few photos

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:15.580
in the input dataset.

00:04:15.580 --> 00:04:20.380
The training still takes from hours to days,
I take this as an interesting detail more

00:04:20.380 --> 00:04:25.680
than a limitation since this training only
has to be done once, and then, using the technique

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:27.620
can take place very quickly.

00:04:27.620 --> 00:04:34.910
But, with that, there you go, a neural algorithm
that understands lighting, geometry, can disentangle

00:04:34.910 --> 00:04:39.790
the two, and reconstruct real-world scenes
from just a few photos.

00:04:39.790 --> 00:04:42.781
It truly feels like we are living in a science
fiction world.

00:04:42.781 --> 00:05:39.440
What a time to be alive!

00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:42.770
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

